  "query_preview": "..The easterly wave that rippled through our area over \nthe past few days has fully departed. Heights aloft are quickly \nbuilding today as the ridge strengthens and expands westward. The \nlocation of the ridge is less optimal for moisture advection into \nNorthern Arizona, however plenty of moisture still lingers with \ndewpoints in the 50s and PW near the 90th percentile. Surface \nheating has been uninhibited this morning under clear skies, and \nscattered showers thunderstorms are firing along terrain features \nas of 1230 MST. The flash flooding threat is lesser than previous \ndays, but still remains the primary hazard given the moist \nprofile, saturated ground conditions, and very slow storm motion. \n\nThe ridge remains strong while moisture continues to trend downward \non Friday. Monsoon activity will be more low-medium grade with \nisolated to scattered storms in the forecast. By late Saturday into \nSunday, the ridge begins to back of a bit and center towards the \nFour Corners. Another complicating factor will be the potential \narrival of an easterly wave associated with the remains of Tropical \nStorm Bertha, though this may follow a trajectory too far south. \nWith heights at 500 mb reaching well above normal through the \nperiod, hot temperatures are expected over the the lower elevations \nof our forecast area. An Extreme Heat Warning will be in effect \nFriday through Sunday for the lower elevations of the Grand Canyon \nand Marble/Glen Canyons.\n\nOn Monday through the middle of next week, the ridge looks to stay \ncentered to our east and the door remains open for good moisture \nadvection into northern AZ.",
